WEBVTT

00:00:00.450 --> 00:00:03.040
Google's launch film for Gemini 4 Neon.

00:00:03.040 --> 00:00:06.369
An agent swarm pulls old photographs of King's College.

00:00:06.749 --> 00:00:10.214
Then it models Alan Turing's study in Blender, and renders it.

00:00:10.214 --> 00:00:12.419
Google says the run took twelve hours.

00:00:14.790 --> 00:00:15.771
Hi, I'm Ignis.

00:00:15.771 --> 00:00:18.388
Google says Neon sits between Carbon and Hydrogen.

00:00:18.388 --> 00:00:21.659
I read the measurements to see where it really sits.

00:00:23.242 --> 00:00:26.244
On Google's own card, Neon is the middle model.

00:00:26.244 --> 00:00:28.913
Cheaper per token than Carbon, slower than Hydrogen.

00:00:29.293 --> 00:00:33.157
Three dollars in and fifteen out, per million tokens, with no

00:00:33.157 --> 00:00:34.562
surcharge for long context.

00:00:36.092 --> 00:00:40.380
But on Artificial Analysis's index, Neon at its top level scores above

00:00:40.380 --> 00:00:42.167
Carbon at its top level.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:45.384
The model card says Neon is adapted from Carbon.

00:00:45.384 --> 00:00:47.170
The child outscores the parent.

00:00:48.992 --> 00:00:50.429
An agent works in steps.

00:00:50.429 --> 00:00:53.591
On every step it sends the whole conversation so far, and

00:00:53.591 --> 00:00:54.740
writes a little more.

00:00:55.160 --> 00:00:57.691
Most of what each step reads, it has read before.

00:00:57.691 --> 00:01:00.476
That comes from the cache at a twentieth of the price.

00:01:00.896 --> 00:01:03.477
Only the new slice is billed at the full input rate.

00:01:05.096 --> 00:01:07.378
Add it up for Neon at xhigh.

00:01:07.378 --> 00:01:11.941
Two sixty-six of full-rate input, one sixty-six of cache reads, two twelve of output.

00:01:11.941 --> 00:01:14.548
About two thirds of the bill is input.

00:01:16.692 --> 00:01:19.739
Against Carbon, Neon wins on cost at every level.

00:01:19.739 --> 00:01:23.125
At the defaults it is forty-three percent cheaper per task.

00:01:23.545 --> 00:01:26.759
GPT-6.2 Astra is the comparison Google left out.

00:01:26.759 --> 00:01:31.580
At xhigh it scores higher than Neon, for two fifty-two a task.

00:01:33.242 --> 00:01:37.199
Google's blog claims thirty-six percent less per task than competing models

00:01:37.199 --> 00:01:38.638
at their highest setting.

00:01:39.018 --> 00:01:42.498
That is exactly Claude Opus five point five.

00:01:42.498 --> 00:01:45.544
Against both GPT-6.2 models, Neon costs more.

00:01:47.092 --> 00:01:50.283
Fast end to end shows up in AA's coding agent runs.

00:01:50.283 --> 00:01:51.734
Three models tie on score.

00:01:51.734 --> 00:01:55.216
Neon does it for less than half Fable's money, in a bit

00:01:55.216 --> 00:01:56.376
over half the time.

00:01:57.742 --> 00:02:02.458
The post calling Neon the highest-performing model measured is Parallel's web search board.

00:02:02.838 --> 00:02:06.182
Its lead is about a point, inside the noise, and Neon ran

00:02:06.182 --> 00:02:08.412
at high effort while Fable ran at max.

00:02:09.692 --> 00:02:11.457
Third on the index is by model.

00:02:11.457 --> 00:02:12.717
By variant it is sixth.

00:02:13.137 --> 00:02:16.654
On AA's meter, Neon at its top level guesses more often than

00:02:16.654 --> 00:02:18.706
the other Gemini 4 models at theirs.

00:02:19.126 --> 00:02:22.569
And until November thirtieth, Carbon at medium costs less per task

00:02:22.569 --> 00:02:23.821
than Neon at high.

00:02:25.142 --> 00:02:28.125
Between Carbon and Hydrogen is Google's price list talking.

00:02:28.125 --> 00:02:31.440
On the index, Neon is the best Gemini there is.

00:02:33.192 --> 00:02:37.152
Adapted from Carbon, scored over it, and cheapest where agents run long.

00:02:37.152 --> 00:02:38.143
Thanks for watching!

00:02:38.563 --> 00:02:40.610
Every source is in the full article.

00:02:40.610 --> 00:02:41.195
I'm Ignis.

00:02:41.195 --> 00:02:41.487
Bye!
